---
import ToolLayout from '../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import { PAGES } from '../lib/pages.ts';
import type { FaqItem } from '../lib/seo.ts';

const faq: FaqItem[] = [
  {
    question: 'How does a roommate chore chart rotation work?',
    answer:
      'A rotation chore chart lists shared household duties and cycles them weekly among housemates. If you clean the kitchen this week, you rotate to the bathroom next week and taking out trash the following week. This prevents resentment because no single roommate gets stuck with the most unpleasant jobs.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the best day to reset and complete roommate chores?',
    answer:
      'Most shared apartments find Sunday evening (such as 6:00 PM or 8:00 PM) works best. It gives everyone the weekend to complete their assigned responsibilities and ensures the house starts the new school or work week clean and organized.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should we do if a roommate consistently misses their chores?',
    answer:
      'Address missed chores early and calmly during a brief house check-in. Agree in advance on house rules: for example, if someone is traveling or swamped with exams, they must arrange a direct chore swap beforehand or cover a shared expense like trash bags or detergent in exchange for coverage.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How should roommates handle cleaning supplies and consumable costs?',
    answer:
      'Set up a shared household kitty or use an expense-splitting app for communal supplies: paper towels, dish soap, mop pads, trash bags, and bathroom cleaners. The roommate assigned to inventory duty flags what is running low each week.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can we share the finished roommate chore chart digitally?',
    answer:
      'Yes. In addition to downloading a clean PDF or PNG for your fridge or hallway, you can click "Copy share link" to send an exact copy into your roommate group chat. Everything stays private in your browser without accounts.',
  },
];

const link = 'text-brand underline font-medium';
---

<ToolLayout page={PAGES.roommateChoreChart} faq={faq}>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    A free printable roommate chore chart maker that rotates duties weekly so housemates share cleaning fairly. Customize names, tasks, and print on Letter or A4 in seconds.
  </Fragment>

  <h2>How a rotating roommate chore chart keeps shared homes peaceful</h2>
  <p>
    A roommate chore chart is the simplest, most effective way to eliminate friction over household cleaning in shared apartments, flats, and student houses. When communal cleaning responsibilities are left unassigned or vague, resentment builds because one person inevitably ends up doing all the dishes or scrubbing the bathroom. A rotating roommate chore chart cycles shared duties automatically every seven days, ensuring everyone contributes an equal share of household labor without anyone having to nag.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because chores rotate in a round-robin schedule each week, no single roommate gets stuck with the most disliked tasks permanently.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works: set up your rotating roommate schedule in 4 steps</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Add housemate names:</strong> Enter names for each roommate sharing communal chores (up to 8 people).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Select shared apartment zones:</strong> Pick from adult household tasks—such as kitchen sanitizing, bathroom scrubbing, vacuuming common areas, taking out trash, or sorting recycling.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choose theme & paper size:</strong> Select a minimalist theme (like Minimal or Classic), pick your reset day (e.g., Sunday evening), and format for Letter or A4 paper.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Print or copy group link:</strong> Download a PDF or PNG to post on the fridge, or click "Copy share link" to drop an exact interactive copy into your roommate group chat.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What makes this roommate chore chart tool different</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Automated fair rotation:</strong> Built-in rotation logic shifts responsibilities every week so everyone shares the heavy jobs equally.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>100% free with no premium paywalls:</strong> All chart types, styling options, and export downloads are completely free.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No account or login required:</strong> You never have to create an account, invite users by email, or download another app.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Private browser storage:</strong> Your house roster and chore assignments stay directly on your device with zero data stored on external servers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Essential chores to include on a shared apartment chart</h2>
  <p>
    Keep roommate duties focused on <strong>common areas</strong> rather than private bedrooms. The most effective roommate charts group chores into 4 to 6 comprehensive weekly zones:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Kitchen deep clean:</strong> Wipe down stove surfaces, clean microwave interior, sanitize countertops, empty sink drain, and run kitchen trash.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Bathroom sanitation:</strong> Scrub toilet bowl and exterior, sanitize sink basin, wipe vanity mirror, wipe shower walls, and replace hand towels.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Living room & hallway floors:</strong> Vacuum carpets and rugs, sweep and mop entryway and hardwood areas, and shake out floor mats.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Trash, recycling & compost:</strong> Empty all shared bins, haul wheelie bins to the curb on collection morning, and replace can liners.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fridge & pantry audit:</strong> Toss expired leftovers, wipe sticky shelves, and compile a quick grocery list for communal essentials like salt, oil, and dish soap.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Four house ground rules that make chore charts work</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Agree on "clean-as-you-go" versus "weekly rotation":</strong> The chore chart does not replace basic daily courtesy. Everyone washes their own dishes after eating and wipes up immediate cooking spills. The rotation covers deep maintenance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Establish a set deadline:</strong> Vague timelines lead to procrastination. Agree that all weekly chores must be finished by Sunday at 7:00 PM before the new rotation begins.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Allow swaps with explicit confirmation:</strong> If a roommate has midterms or travels for the weekend, they are free to swap with a housemate, but they are responsible for arranging the trade in advance.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep supplies stocked together:</strong> Nothing stalls bathroom cleaning faster than an empty bottle of spray cleaner. Pool five dollars each per semester for cleaning supplies or rotate buying communal essentials.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Related printable tools for households and families</h2>
  <p>
    Need other printable organizational schedules? Explore our free companion makers:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={PAGES.choreChartMaker.path} class={link}>Chore Chart Maker</a>: the full tool for custom daily, weekly, or reward routines.</li>
    <li><a href={PAGES.familyChoreChart.path} class={link}>Family Chore Chart</a>: organize tasks for households with children and parents.</li>
    <li><a href={PAGES.morningRoutineChart.path} class={link}>Morning Routine Chart</a>: visual checklists for smooth morning habits.</li>
    <li><a href={PAGES.bedtimeRoutineChart.path} class={link}>Bedtime Routine Chart</a>: structured evening checklists for calm nights.</li>
    <li><a href={PAGES.elegantChoreChart.path} class={link}>Elegant Chore Chart</a>: minimalist monochrome charts designed to look great framed on a wall.</li>
  </ul>
</ToolLayout>
